Task 1: Install and Configure HashiCorp Vault
Subtask 1.1: Install Vault Binary
First, we need to install HashiCorp Vault on our Linux system.

# Update system packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required dependencies
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl gpg

# Download Vault binary (latest stable version)
cd /tmp
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip

# Verify the download
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_SHA256SUMS
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_SHA256SUMS.sig

# Extract and install Vault
unzip vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/vault

# Verify installation
vault version
Subtask 1.2: Create Vault Configuration
Create a configuration file for Vault server operation.

# Create Vault directory structure
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/data
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d

# Create Vault configuration file
sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
EOF

# Set appropriate permissions
sudo chown -R vault:vault /opt/vault /etc/vault.d 2>/dev/null || true
sudo chmod 640 /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
Subtask 1.3: Create Vault Service User
# Create vault user for service
sudo useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault

# Set ownership
sudo chown -R vault:vault /opt/vault /etc/vault.d
Subtask 1.4: Start Vault Server
# Start Vault server in development mode for learning
# Note: In production, you would use a systemd service
vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl &

# Wait for server to start
